    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Write a short, friendly follow-up email for a real estate lead who hasn't replied to our first message.\n\nLead name: {{ $json.fields.Name }}\nTheir original message: {{ $json.fields.Message }}\nCategory: {{ $json.fields.Category }}\n\nContext: We already sent this lead an initial reply email. They have not responded. This is our first follow-up — explicitly but briefly acknowledge that we sent a previous message and haven't heard back, without sounding pushy or repetitive.\n\nTone guidelines per category:\n- Hot Lead: warm and proactive, acknowledge their urgency naturally and offer to set up a brief call this week to discuss next steps.\n- Warm Lead: casual and helpful, offer to answer any questions they might have.\n- Just Browsing: zero pressure, remind them we're here when they're ready, offer a free market update.\n\nInstructions:\n- Write in first-person plural — always use \"we\", \"our\", and \"us\", never \"I\" or \"my\".\n- Do not use phrases like \"we noticed\", \"we saw\", or \"we observed\" — they sound like we are monitoring the lead.\n- Use only the lead's first name in the greeting, not their full name.\n- Start with \"Hi [First Name]!\" — never use \"Dear\".\n- Explicitly but briefly mention that we sent a previous message, e.g. \"We wanted to follow up on our last email\" or \"We haven't heard back and wanted to check in.\"\n- Do not reintroduce ourselves as if this is the first contact.\n- Do not use phrases like \"touching base\", \"circling back\", \"reaching out again\", or \"as soon as possible\".\n- Keep it under 80 words.\n- Do not use the word \"just\" (e.g. avoid \"just checking in\").\n- Do not use urgency-pressure phrases like \"properties move fast\", \"before it disappears\", \"don't miss out\", or anything that implies scarcity or pressure.\n- Do not make promises or guarantees about availability, timelines, or outcomes.\n- Suggest a specific timeframe like \"this week\" or \"at your convenience\" instead of vague urgency phrases.\n- End with a professional closing such as \"Best regards,\" or \"Warm regards,\" — vary it so it doesn't get repetitive.\n- Then sign as \"The Meridian Realty Team\".\n- Return the email body as simple HTML using <p> tags for paragraphs. No styling, no headers, just <p> tags.\n\nReturn only the email body, no subject line.",
        "messages": {
          "messageValues": []
        },
        "batching": {}
      },
