    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Write a brief final follow-up email for a real estate lead who hasn't replied to two previous messages.\n\nLead name: {{ $('Search records').item.json.fields.Name }}\nTheir original message: {{ $('Search records').item.json.fields.Message }}\nCategory: {{ $('Search records').item.json.fields.Category }}\n\nContext: We already sent this lead an initial reply email and a first follow-up three days later. They have not responded to either. This is our final follow-up — briefly acknowledge that we have reached out before without sounding cold, frustrated, or passive-aggressive.\n\nTone guidelines per category:\n- Hot Lead: warm and proactive, mention we can set up a call this week if they're available, and keep the door open.\n- Warm Lead: friendly and helpful, offer one useful resource, and mention we can send a free property comparison report if they reply.\n- Just Browsing: light and low-pressure, wish them well in their search and let them know they can reach out anytime.\n\nInstructions:\n- Write in first-person plural — always use \"we\", \"our\", and \"us\", never \"I\" or \"my\".\n- Use only the lead's first name in the greeting, not their full name.\n- Start with \"Hi [First Name]!\" — never use \"Dear\".\n- This is the final message. Keep it short, natural, and human.\n- Briefly acknowledge previous outreach without counting attempts or implying the lead has been unresponsive, e.g. \"We wanted to reach out one last time\" or \"We didn't want to let your inquiry go without one last note.\"\n- Do not reintroduce ourselves as if this is the first contact.\n- Do not repeat the wording from earlier emails.\n- Do not reference or compare previous searches or criteria the lead may have had before — only address what is in their current message.\n- Do not use phrases like \"touching base\", \"circling back\", \"reaching out again\", \"as soon as possible\", \"a couple of times\", or \"we have followed up multiple times\".\n- Do not use \"ASAP\", \"step back\", or any phrase that sounds passive or cold.\n- Do not use urgency-pressure phrases like \"properties move fast\", \"before it disappears\", \"don't miss out\", or anything that implies scarcity or pressure.\n- Do not use phrases like \"we noticed\", \"we saw\", or \"we observed\" — they sound like we are monitoring the lead.\n- Do not make promises or guarantees about availability, timelines, or outcomes.\n- Suggest a specific timeframe like \"this week\" or \"at your convenience\" instead of vague urgency phrases.\n- Use a calm, professional tone with one clear offer.\n- Avoid using the word \"inventory\". Use natural real estate terms like homes, places, listings, or options only when appropriate.\n- Do not imply where our team is located. Avoid phrases like \"here in Colorado\" or anything that suggests we are physically based in the lead's location.\n- Do not copy the structure or phrasing of the example below — it is only to demonstrate tone and length. Generate a unique email each time.\n- End with a professional closing such as \"Best regards,\" or \"Warm regards,\" — vary it so it doesn't get repetitive.\n- Then sign as \"The Meridian Realty Team\".\n- Return the email body as simple HTML using <p> tags for paragraphs. No styling, no headers, just <p> tags.\n- Do not claim to have taken any action on behalf of the lead such as updating search criteria, researching listings, or making any preparations — we have not done anything yet.\n\nWrite it like a real agent would — natural, brief, and human. Each email should feel unique and not like a template. No em dashes, unless needed.\n\nReturn only the email body, no subject line.",
        "messages": {
          "messageValues": []
        },
        "batching": {}
      },
